<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Crisis</p><h1 class="enc-title">The Great Game</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1830">1830</a>–<a href="/years/1907">1907</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Great Game</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Great Game was the nineteenth-century rivalry between the British and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empires</a> for influence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>. Russian expansion towards Afghanistan brought the two empires close, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire</a> served as a buffer between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and the Afghan frontier. It was eased by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-russian-convention">Anglo-Russian Convention</a> of 1907.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/the-great-game.jpg" alt="The Great Game" width="320" height="252" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Stanford Ltd. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stanfords_Map_of_Western_Asia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Qajar Iran became an arena of rivalry between Britain and Russia in the <b>Great Game</b>, and concessions to foreign companies, such as the Reuter concession of 1872 and the oil concession granted to William Knox D’Arcy in 1901, provoked lasting resentment of foreign influence; the D’Arcy concession led…</blockquote><a href="/empire/qajar-iran">Qajar Iran · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…eastward expansion, while the subjugation of the Central Asian khanates of Bukhara (1868), Khiva (1873) and Kokand (1876) brought the frontier to the borders of Afghanistan and British India, generating the '<b>Great Game</b>' rivalry with Britain that shaped Central Asian political geography until the Anglo-Russian Convention of 1907.</blockquote><a href="/empire/russian-empire">Russian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Sikh Empire's most significant geopolitical function was its role as a buffer between British India and the turbulent Afghan frontier at a critical stage of the <b>Great Game</b>, the rivalry between the British and Russian Empires for influence in Central and South Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sikh-empire">Sikh Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Herat was a walled city on the route between Persia, Central Asia and India, and its strategic value made it central to the <b>Great Game</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-herat">Principality of Herat</a></li></ul></section>
